<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norddeutscher Lloyd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as North German Lloyd</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norddeutscher Lloyd was a shipping company founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> in 1857. Operating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremerhaven">Bremerhaven</a>, it carried millions of emigrants to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and became one of the largest shipping companies in the world, winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-riband">Blue Riband</a> with liners such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ss-kaiser-wilhelm-der-grosse">Kaiser Wilhelm der Grosse</a> and the Bremen. In 1970 it merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-america-line">Hamburg America Line</a> to form Hapag-Lloyd.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> merchants Hermann Henrich Meier and Eduard Crüsemann founded the company on 20 February 1857, after the dissolution of the German-American Ocean Steam Navigation Company. Meier chaired the supervisory board and Crüsemann became its first director. Its first ship, the Adler, sailed from Nordenham to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 28 October 1857, and a year later regular services ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremerhaven">Bremerhaven</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with the steamships Bremen and New York. The company lost money until 1859, then added Baltimore, in partnership with the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, and bought waterfront facilities at Hoboken, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, in 1869.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Johann Georg Lohmann, director from 1877, the company built fast liners, and in 1885 it won the German imperial mail contract to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the Far East. By 1890 it was the second largest shipping company in the world after P&amp;O and carried more transatlantic passengers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> than any other, mostly emigrants in steerage. The Kaiser Wilhelm der Große of 1897, then the largest and fastest ship afloat, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-riband">Blue Riband</a>, as did the Kronprinz Wilhelm and Kaiser Wilhelm II in 1902 and 1904. On 30 June 1900 over 300 dock workers and others died in a fire at Hoboken. By 1914 it employed about 22,000 people.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> took every ship over 1,600 GRT, leaving the company with 57,000 GRT. It rebuilt, helped found Deutscher Aero Lloyd, a forerunner of Lufthansa, and in 1929 and 1930 launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and Europa, both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-riband">Blue Riband</a> winners. Hit by the Depression, it was placed in trusteeship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-america-line">HAPAG</a> in 1932, and under the Nazi regime the National Socialist Rudolph Firle headed it. The whole fleet was lost in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> or handed over as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>; the Steuben was sunk in the Baltic in 1945 with the loss of some 4,000 lives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Relicensed by the American military administration on 29 November 1945 with 350 employees, the company built a new fleet from 1951, resumed North Atlantic passenger service in 1955 and opened a container service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1968. Already doing three quarters of its freight business with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-america-line">HAPAG</a>, it merged with it on 1 September 1970 to form Hapag-Lloyd, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. The Lloyd Werft in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremerhaven">Bremerhaven</a>, the Lloyd Passage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and the Bremer Bank, founded by Meier, keep its memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norddeutscher Lloyd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
